MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Gonçalo,

you could treat study areas as strata, i.e., introduce into your analysis a factor that indexes each study area. That way, you would accommodate the constant correlation in occupancy and detection for different sites within the same study site. This model can easily be fitted in unmarked.

Nowadays, such a block, or stratum, effect would typically be treated as a random effect. You can't do this in unmarked, but this would be easy in BUGS for instance.

Either model would not accommodate the potential distance-dependent correlation between sites within the same study area. For this, you would have to fit a model that does accommodate spatial autocorrelation, for instance, a CAR model. This cannot be done in unmarked, and you would again have to go the BUGS way. Chelgren et al., Ecology, 2011, is a reference for this.

I think I would be happy with with the first approach, but would check the GOF test which is available for static occupancy models in package AICcmodavg. However, if you burn to do some BUGS modeling, then either solution two or three is reasonably straightforward.

Kind regards  --- Marc

